\begin{frame}{Two Ways to Relate to Models}
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{center}
\textbf{\large Machine Learning}
\vspace{0.5em}

\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{figures/honest_work.jpg}
\end{center}

\begin{itemize}
\item Output-oriented
\item Find hyperparameters to improve models
\end{itemize}
\end{column}

\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{center}
\textbf{\large Mech.\ Interpretability}
\vspace{0.5em}

\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{figures/scientist_microscope.jpg}
\end{center}

\begin{itemize}
\item Learned models are the object of study
\item Understand and reverse-engineer parameters
\end{itemize}
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item Two ways to relate to neural networks
\item The standard ML view: models are tools, output is what matters, tune hyperparameters until they work
\item The mech interp view: the learned model is the object of study, treat it like a biologist treats an organism
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[plain]
\vfill
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth,height=0.9\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/micrographia.jpg}
\end{center}
\vfill
\hfill{\tiny From \cite{olah2020}}\quad

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item Normally in ML, we look at the algorithms we use to create neural networks: architectures, optimizers, hyperparameters, training data
\item We actively make decisions about these, so it makes sense to focus on them
\item Chris Olah's idea: look at the neural networks that come out of ML as objects of scientific inquiry
\item Similar to biologists not just studying evolution, but studying organisms by looking inside them
\item People tried to understand how life works for a long time
\item Ancient Greeks: the body is made of 4 humors that must be balanced
\item Doctors for centuries tried bloodletting and purging --- not crowned by success
\item The problem: wrong ontology. Understanding humors is not helpful for understanding disease
\item The right ontology came with the microscope: organisms are made of cells
\item Study cells, their properties, what makes them work, what bacteria cause which sicknesses --- then you get somewhere
\item Olah's vision: do for ML what the microscope did for biology
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Schwann's Claims (1839)}
\begin{columns}[c]
\begin{column}{0.6\textwidth}
\begin{enumerate}
\item The cell is the unit of structure, physiology, and organization in living things.

\vspace{0.5em}
\item The cell retains a dual existence as a distinct entity and a building block in the construction of organisms.

\vspace{0.5em}
\item \textcolor{gray}{Cells form by free-cell formation, similar to the formation of crystals.}
\end{enumerate}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.35\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/schwann_book.jpg}

{\centering\tiny Image: Deutsches Textarchiv\par}
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item Theodor Schwann described what cells are and how they work
\item These three claims lay down an ontology for cellular biology as a field
\item Even though Claim 3 turned out to be wrong, the first two claims defined the field
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Three Speculative Claims}
\begin{columns}[c]
\begin{column}{0.6\textwidth}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Features} are the fundamental unit of neural networks.
They correspond to directions.
These features can be rigorously studied and understood.

\vspace{0.5em}
\item Features are connected by weights, forming \textbf{circuits}.
These circuits can also be rigorously studied and understood.

\vspace{0.5em}
\item \textbf{Universality}: Analogous features and circuits form across models and tasks.
\end{enumerate}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.35\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/activation_atlas_book.png}
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\source{olah2020}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item In his 2020 essay ``Zoom In'', Olah lays down similar claims to define a tentative ontology for MechInterp
\item These are not only descriptive, but normative
\item Claim 1: a feature is a fundamental unit, corresponding to a direction in activation space. The claim is that individual directions can be understood, and they are fundamental units
\item Question to audience: how would the world look if this claim were false? (possible: binary encoding, context-dependent meanings)
\item Claim 2: features connected by weights form circuits that can be understood
\item Question: what if Claim 1 is true but Claim 2 is false? (possible: features are readable storage but the process itself is more complicated, like CoT being readable but the LLM's use of it being inscrutable)
\item Claim 3: universality
\item If false: we could not learn from one network to another, every new NN would have completely different features and circuits
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{}
\begin{center}
\Large How would MechInterp help AI safety?
\end{center}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item Now, what if those claims turn out to be true, how would that help with AI safety?
\item Question to audience, collecting ideas
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Theories of Impact}
\begin{itemize}
\item Auditing for misalignment/deception
\item Enumerative safety
\item Eliciting latent knowledge
\end{itemize}

\source{nanda2022}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item Auditing: if we can find features/circuits for misaligned motivation, cheating, thinking about bioweapons, etc., we could detect this before or during deployment
\item Enumerative safety: if we can identify the function of every part of a neural network, we can be sure it does not contain bad intentions or dangerous capabilities
\item Eliciting latent knowledge: we can find out more directly what the model knows
\item Helps with transparency, against scheming
\item Also helps with ``AI for science'': e.g.\ a genetics model that knows some genetic marker is responsible for some effect, but since it is not a language model, you cannot just ask it
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Why CNNs?}
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth,height=0.75\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/cnn_diagram.png}
\end{center}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item Most early MechInterp work was done on visual neural networks, typically CNNs
\item Many early impressive neural networks were visual neural networks (e.g.\ AlexNet)
\item MechInterp is easier with convolutional networks: the different channels of a convolutional layer are a preferred basis, which makes the directions that might be features architecturally clear
\item There is a lot of universality in vision CNNs, compared to transformer-based LLMs
\item As we will see, feature visualization works pretty well in vision models
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{}
\begin{center}
\Large What would convince you a CNN channel has the function of ``detecting a curve?''
\end{center}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item Discussion: what evidence would we demand for that claim?
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Argument 1: Feature Visualization}
\begin{columns}[c]
\begin{column}{0.35\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/curve_detector_feature_visualization.png}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.6\textwidth}
Optimizing the input to maximally activate curve detectors reliably produces curves.
This establishes a causal link.
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\source{olah2020}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item Optimizing the input to cause curve detectors to fire reliably produces curves
\item This establishes a causal link, since everything in the resulting image was added to cause the neuron to fire more
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Argument 2: Dataset Examples}
\begin{columns}[c]
\begin{column}{0.4\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/curve_detector_dataset_examples.png}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.55\textwidth}
ImageNet images that strongly activate these neurons reliably contain curves in the expected orientation.
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\source{olah2020}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item The ImageNet images that cause these neurons to strongly fire are reliably curves in the expected orientation
\item Images that cause moderate firing are generally less perfect curves or curves off orientation
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Argument 3: Synthetic Examples}
\begin{columns}[c]
\begin{column}{0.4\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/curve_detector_synthetic_tuning.png}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.55\textwidth}
Curve detectors respond as expected to synthetic curves with varying orientations and curvatures.
They do not fire for straight lines or sharp corners.
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\source{olah2020}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item Curve detectors respond as expected to a range of synthetic curve images created with varying orientations, curvatures, and backgrounds
\item They fire only near the expected orientation, and do not fire strongly for straight lines or sharp corners
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Argument 4: Joint Tuning}
\begin{columns}[c]
\begin{column}{0.35\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/curve_detector_radial_tuning.png}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.6\textwidth}
Rotating a stimulus smoothly transfers activation between orientation-selective detectors.
Together, they tile the full 360 degrees.
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\source{olah2020}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item If we take dataset examples that cause a neuron to fire and rotate them, they gradually stop firing and the curve detector in the next orientation begins firing
\item This shows they detect rotated versions of the same thing
\item Together, they tile the full 360 degrees of potential orientations
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Argument 5: Feature Implementation}
\begin{columns}[c]
\begin{column}{0.4\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/curve_detector_weights.png}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.55\textwidth}
A curve detection algorithm can be read off the weights.
No evidence of a secondary cause of firing.
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\source{olah2020}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item Circuit-based argument
\item By looking at the circuit constructing the curve detectors, we can read a curve detection algorithm off of the weights
\item We don't see anything suggestive of a second alternative cause of firing
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Argument 6: Feature Use}
\begin{columns}[c]
\begin{column}{0.35\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/curve_detector_use_in_circuits.png}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.6\textwidth}
Downstream clients are features that naturally involve curves: circles, spirals, 3d curvature.
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\source{olah2020}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item Circuit-based argument
\item The downstream clients of curve detectors are features that naturally involve curves (circles, 3d curvature, spirals)
\item The curve detectors are used by these clients in the expected manner
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Argument 7: Handwritten Circuits}
\begin{columns}[c]
\begin{column}{0.35\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/arg_hand.png}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.6\textwidth}
We can do a cleanroom reimplementation, hand-setting all weights to reimplement curve detection.
These handwritten weights significantly mimic the original.
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\source{olah2020}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item Circuit-based argument
\item Based on our understanding of how curve detectors are implemented, we can do a cleanroom reimplementation
\item Hand setting all weights to reimplement curve detection
\item These weights are an understandable curve detection algorithm, and significantly mimic the original curve detectors
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{A Complex Feature: Cars in Superposition}
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth,height=0.75\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/car_circuit.png}
\end{center}

\source{olah2020}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item These methods also work to identify more complex structures
\item Note that these are still human-understandable
\item We can even understand the circuit here
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Universality in Image Models}
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth,height=0.8\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/universality.png}
\end{center}

\source{olah2020}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item In image models, these features at least in early layers seem to be pretty universal
\item Curve detectors and high-low frequency detectors appear across AlexNet, GoogLeNet, VGG19, and ResNet
\item Later layers often correspond to human concepts, which speaks for their universality
\item But many channels do not seem to have easily understandable descriptions
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Polysemanticity}
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth,height=0.7\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/superposition.png}
\end{center}

\source{olah2020}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item One problem, that we will discuss later, but is already visible in these models
\item Sometimes a feature is split up across different neurons, and multiple features share one neuron
\item Here we see that the car feature is assembled in one layer, but does not get its own neuron in a later layer
\item Its activations are split and added onto a bunch of other neurons that encode unrelated things
\item We can already see that this is necessary when we want to have more features than we have neurons
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{If Universality Is True}
\begin{itemize}
\item What features are universal? Why?
\item Discussed in the Natural Abstractions discourse
\end{itemize}

\source{wentworth2020, chan2023}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item For curves this is obvious, but it is not clear how far this universality scales
\item Even if it does, it is not clear why those concepts in particular are universal
\item This might be pretty important: if we find a ``morally good'' feature in LLMs, do we expect it to behave the same as the human concept OOD in the limit of a super capable model?
\item Unclear, there are some theories here. AFAIK the work in this direction has stalled out the last years
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

